WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:01.668
In this lesson,

2
00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:05.205
you'll learn how Outlines is able
to be so efficient in this generation

3
00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:09.409
and see how structure generation
allows for structure far beyond JSON.

4
00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:12.946
You'll understand
how regular expressions can be transformed

5
00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:15.949
into finite state machines
for efficient computation.

6
00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:20.020
Finally, it looks for various examples
of structure beyond JSON.

7
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.988
Let's have some fun.

8
00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:25.392
In the last lesson we talked about
how outlines is able to modify

9
00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:28.895
the logits to guarantee the output
of the generation from the model.

10
00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:30.196
In this lesson,

11
00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:31.865
we're going to dive a bit deeper
and understand

12
00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:34.834
exactly how Outlines is able
to keep track of the structure.

13
00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:37.170
Outlines uses regular expressions
under the hood.

14
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.473
To model the structure
we want to use. This allows us to define

15
00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:44.444
a much wider range of structure
than just JSON alone.

16
00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:46.012
There's also an interesting relationship

17
00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:48.782
between regular expressions
and finite state machines.

18
00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:52.385
We actually use this to take advantage
of a way to easily

19
00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.354
and efficiently process the structure

20
00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:55.522
as we go through the model.

21
00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:57.123
First, let's talk a little bit

22
00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.092
about what regular expressions are
and how they work.

23
00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.661
You probably are familiar with them,
but just in case you're not.

24
00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:02.896
Let's do a quick refresher.

25
00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:05.965
This here is a very simple
regular expression that describes

26
00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:08.568
the example
we talked about in the very first lesson.

27
00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:11.571
This is a series of A, B
and C's that have to be in order.

28
00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:14.741
Now, there can be any number of A's
including zero,

29
00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:16.810
any number of B's, any number of C's

30
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.178
and then the end of the string.

31
00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:20.814
So, valid strings
here would be the empty string

32
00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:22.282
which doesn't violate our constraints.

33
00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.983
Has no letters, but that's fine.

34
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.687
ABC of course is an order AA, B, CCC.

35
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.923
That's also fine. Repetition is allowed.

36
00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.825
And of course C by itself is also allowed

37
00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:35.395
because none of the characters
before it are in violation of our regex.

38
00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:39.833
Invalid strings would be BA, AAABBBCCB.

39
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:41.468
It doesn't matter
how many times we're in order.

40
00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:44.838
If we're not in order once,
it won't be valid and CBA is

41
00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:46.539
of course, obviously not a valid string.

42
00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.141
So let's talk about this relationship

43
00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.610
between regular expressions
and finite state machines.

44
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.445
We can actually take this
regular expression

45
00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:55.482
that we just described and transform it
into this finite state machine

46
00:01:55.482 --> 00:01:56.649
you see here on the right.

47
00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:59.619
Now, you may think this is just adding
complexity to our problem,

48
00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:01.521
but this finite state machine actually

49
00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:05.692
makes it very easy for programs
to keep track of the regular expression.

50
00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:09.462
Let's walk through a pattern matching
example using this finite state machine.

51
00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:13.366
So, given our finite state machine that
represents the regex we described earlier,

52
00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:17.871
we have this string AABC
and we like to see if it matches.

53
00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:21.274
So we start in it
start position and we observe in A.

54
00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.410
You can see observing this
A leads us to the A state.

55
00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:27.247
Now that we're in the A state,
we observe another A.

56
00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:29.482
See how we loop back into the A state

57
00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.417
again? We remain in the A state.

58
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.052
Next, we observe a B.

59
00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:36.089
This moves us from the
A state to the B state.

60
00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:40.693
Now we observe a C and we get to talk
about what it means to match a string.

61
00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:44.831
When we match a string, that means
we successfully end in the goal state

62
00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:48.701
marked by two circles in this diagram,
and we have no more string to process.

63
00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:51.604
So here we are
successfully matching this string.

64
00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:54.440
Now, let's step through an example
where we fail to match.

65
00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.243
This string is invalid
according to our regular expression.

66
00:02:57.243 --> 00:03:00.246
Let's see how a finite state
machine can be used to prove that.

67
00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:05.185
So again we start in our start state and
we observe in A moving us to the A state.

68
00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:08.221
Next, we observe a C
which moves us to the C state.

69
00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:09.522
So far so good.

70
00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:12.492
But we also haven't violated
any of the rules of our regex.

71
00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:17.492
Now we have an A, but as you can see,
none of the paths leading out of C have an

72
00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:22.068
A, and we are not in our end state,
so we have failed to match.

73
00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:25.405
So you may be asking
how is this finite state

74
00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:30.343
machine used to match regular expressions
useful for generating structure?

75
00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:35.448
Well, we can just invert our process
when we are using the FSM with Outlines,

76
00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.116
this is how it works behind the scenes.

77
00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:37.650
We are keeping

78
00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.321
track of what state we're in
and looking at what paths from that state.

79
00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:45.391
So here we are in the start state, and
of course, all possible paths are allowed.

80
00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:47.126
Here's our logits on the right.

81
00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:50.697
And we can see the structure doesn't
require any changes to those logits,

82
00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:53.166
since all of the tokens
we have are admissible.

83
00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:55.969
But we admit a B
and we move to the B state.

84
00:03:55.969 --> 00:03:58.838
Now here you can see
we don't have all the paths.

85
00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:02.542
So, even though the model produces
logit weights for all the options,

86
00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:06.746
we know that only paths
B C, and A are allowed.

87
00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:09.749
And we continue this on
as we move through our generation.

88
00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:10.850
So in this way,

89
00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.887
we've been able to invert the process
of matching into one of generating.

90
00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:18.424
Regex is a remarkable,
powerful way to represent structure.

91
00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:21.160
There is much, much more to it
than just JSON.

92
00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.462
Simple structure is often

93
00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:26.065
enough for most tasks
that we have, for example, class labels.

94
00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:29.969
If you're building a quick 0 or 1 shot
classifier, you may want to limit

95
00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:31.671
the output of your model to the class
labels

96
00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:34.240
you have. No need for JSON on top of that.

97
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.242
Email addresses are another example.

98
00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:38.978
If you are parsing our email addresses
from documents,

99
00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:42.215
it would be nice if the LLM could
consistently output email addresses.

100
00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:45.385
Likewise, phone numbers
can be a very useful regular expression

101
00:04:45.385 --> 00:04:48.421
to make sure that the output of the LLM
matches the phone number.

102
00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:52.025
This is also important because people can
write phone numbers in different ways.

103
00:04:52.025 --> 00:04:54.260
So you may want to have
a consistent format

104
00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.262
that is applied
when phone numbers are parsed.

105
00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:58.598
You can also output any common document
types.

106
00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:01.567
We've talked a lot about JSON,
but of course it's worth pointing out

107
00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:05.605
we can still produce JSON,
but we can also write CSV files or Yaml.

108
00:05:05.672 --> 00:05:08.241
They can also be used to represent
context-free grammars.

109
00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:11.277
Now, for the formal language enthusiasts
watching this course,

110
00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:12.779
you may see a surprise here.

111
00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:15.048
Ordinarily,
we think of context-free grammars

112
00:05:15.048 --> 00:05:17.884
as explicitly more powerful
than regular expressions.

113
00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:18.518
However,

114
00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:22.455
we can represent any arbitrary context-free
grammar as a regular expression

115
00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:25.291
so long as we limit
the depth of recursion we allow.

116
00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:28.561
Now, once we have context-free grammars,
we can have syntactically

117
00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:31.597
correct programing languages as output,
which is a very impressive thing.

118
00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:34.834
Also, you can basically model
any document type you can imagine.

119
00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:36.769
There's all sorts of structure

120
00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:39.772
out there in the world and with context
free grammars available to you,

121
00:05:39.806 --> 00:05:42.375
there's really no limit
to what you can represent.

122
00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:44.444
Now, let's go see some examples.

123
00:05:44.444 --> 00:05:46.512
We're going to start by adding
these two lines of code

124
00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:49.015
we've been adding. So that we don't see
any unnecessary warnings.

125
00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:51.384
Next, we're going to add these libraries
we'll be using.

126
00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:54.220
We have a utility template
that helps us write prompts easily.

127
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:55.388
We'll also be using Outlines

128
00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:56.422
And we're going to be using

129
00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:59.592
the greedy sample from Outlines
in some cases to make sure we always know

130
00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:01.494
exactly what output
we'll be getting from the model.

131
00:06:01.494 --> 00:06:04.263
Next, we'll load our LLM.
We're going to be using HuggingFace's

132
00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:06.199
SmolLM2

133
00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:09.469
The 135 million Instruct version.

134
00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:13.072
This runs very well even on very resource
constrained hardware.

135
00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:15.842
So all these examples
will be actually running on a CPU

136
00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:17.243
with about eight gigabytes of RAM.

137
00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:19.212
A very impressive accomplishment.

138
00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:21.714
The first structure we're going to look at
is a choice.

139
00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:22.749
Making a choice is use

140
00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:26.219
when we want to label something
with a finite number of possible labels.

141
00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:29.555
So in this case, we have a simple request
where we want to classify

142
00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.958
restaurant reviews
either positive or negative.

143
00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:33.826
This is a very common pattern for any time

144
00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:37.363
we're doing, an one shot
or zero-shot classifier with an LLM.

145
00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.032
So we just simply want a class label
to be omitted.

146
00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.868
There's actually two ways
we can solve that.

147
00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:43.603
Since we just talked
about regular expressions, it's

148
00:06:43.603 --> 00:06:46.539
worth pointing out that this can be solved
with a regular expression.

149
00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:49.909
Here's an example of a regular expression
that represents this.

150
00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:53.479
So there's only two possible
strings allowed positive or negative.

151
00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:56.549
Now that's not too bad,
but it's kind of tedious to have to write

152
00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:58.518
regular expressions
for a more complex model.

153
00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:02.188
So we're going to actually
be using something else called choice.

154
00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:05.892
Here is the code using Outline's choice
to create a generator

155
00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:08.094
that will only choose between positive
and negative.

156
00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:08.628
In this code,

157
00:07:08.628 --> 00:07:10.830
you can see
rather than passing the full regex,

158
00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.799
we just have to pass
in a list of the values

159
00:07:12.799 --> 00:07:16.102
we want to be emitted from the model,
in this case positive and negative.

160
00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:18.070
You can add
as many of these as you'd like,

161
00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.606
and this is certainly easier
than writing a regular expression.

162
00:07:20.606 --> 00:07:23.509
It also allows us to get labels
from another source

163
00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:26.012
and pumped them directly into the model,
which can be very useful.

164
00:07:26.012 --> 00:07:30.116
Finally, we will run our new model
we have and see what we get.

165
00:07:30.116 --> 00:07:32.618
And the result is
that the review was positive.

166
00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:33.653
Of course, the reviewer said

167
00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:36.823
that the pizza was delicious
and we get the result we were looking for.

168
00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.525
And this next example,
we'll be looking at a phone number

169
00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.461
regular expression, for this task,

170
00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:44.464
we want to extract a phone number from,
a prompt.

171
00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:48.100
Now, if you look at this case,
we actually want to extract the phone

172
00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:52.338
number in a different format than it's
provided in our format requirements.

173
00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:55.575
We would like to have parentheses
around the area code, a space.

174
00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:59.278
The first three local numbers and then
separated by a dash the last four.

175
00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:01.848
Here's an example of the number provided
which doesn't match to this.

176
00:08:01.848 --> 00:08:04.484
This is a very, very useful application.

177
00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:07.220
A very simple structure
for problems for data extraction.

178
00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.322
Here's a regular expression
you'll be using for this.

179
00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:10.890
As you can see, it encodes

180
00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.992
all the properties of the phone number
we just described.

181
00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:16.562
Now, all we have to do is take our regex
and use

182
00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:19.765
outline dot generate dot regex
to create a phone number generator.

183
00:08:19.799 --> 00:08:23.236
This will extract phone numbers for us
in exactly the format we were hoping for.

184
00:08:23.369 --> 00:08:26.839
When we run this, we can see that it
pretty successfully pulled the number out

185
00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:29.041
we needed
and it applied the format we wanted.

186
00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:31.978
Compare for a moment different formats
we have in the prompt.

187
00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:34.680
There was a dash between the area code
and no parentheses,

188
00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:38.184
and our generator was successfully able
to pull out the correct format.

189
00:08:38.718 --> 00:08:41.487
Next, we'll do the same thing
with email addresses.

190
00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:43.756
In this case, we will definitely want
a regular expression for this

191
00:08:43.756 --> 00:08:45.825
because email addresses
can be very complicated.

192
00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:48.694
Now if you know something about how email
addresses can be validated,

193
00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:51.230
this is actually not the fully perfect
email address.

194
00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:52.665
But it works for our case.

195
00:08:52.665 --> 00:08:54.867
For this example,
we're just going to ask our model

196
00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:58.104
to generate us a random email address
for someone at Amazon.

197
00:08:58.104 --> 00:09:01.407
Once again, we just define a simple regex
generator using outlines.

198
00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:04.010
And generate the email address.

199
00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:06.345
Next, we're going to do something
a bit more sophisticated.

200
00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:09.649
We'll be using a regular expression
to generate an HTML image tag

201
00:09:09.749 --> 00:09:11.484
based on a file name we provide to it.

202
00:09:11.484 --> 00:09:13.119
Since we're working
with a more complicated

203
00:09:13.119 --> 00:09:14.153
regular expression,

204
00:09:14.153 --> 00:09:16.989
I want to introduce a technique
for working with structured generation

205
00:09:16.989 --> 00:09:18.124
that can be very helpful.

206
00:09:18.124 --> 00:09:22.161
Here we have an example of the structure
we're hoping to get out of the model.

207
00:09:22.361 --> 00:09:26.632
This is a very, very useful technique
for making sure that the structure

208
00:09:26.632 --> 00:09:28.267
you're defining is correct.

209
00:09:28.267 --> 00:09:30.303
And since this is a non-trivial case,
we really want

210
00:09:30.303 --> 00:09:33.005
to make sure our regex works.
Let's take a look at the regex

211
00:09:33.005 --> 00:09:35.541
we're going to be using.
Here is our image tag regex.

212
00:09:35.541 --> 00:09:36.642
This looks good to me,

213
00:09:36.642 --> 00:09:40.046
but I would like to be able to verify
that this actually matches our example

214
00:09:40.279 --> 00:09:42.248
before we run our model.

215
00:09:42.248 --> 00:09:46.185
We're going to import
Python's regular Expression library, re.

216
00:09:46.519 --> 00:09:48.621
And we're going to search
for our image tag.

217
00:09:48.621 --> 00:09:53.025
In the example we have. As you can see, it
successfully found a match.

218
00:09:53.292 --> 00:09:55.461
This is really strong evidence
that we have to find

219
00:09:55.461 --> 00:09:57.663
the correct
regular expression for our task,

220
00:09:57.663 --> 00:10:01.500
and saves us a lot of time finding errors
when we actually running our LLM.

221
00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:04.003
Since we can trust
that our structure looks good, it's

222
00:10:04.003 --> 00:10:05.438
now time to build our generator.

223
00:10:05.438 --> 00:10:07.974
Now we're using our generator
to create the image tag.

224
00:10:07.974 --> 00:10:11.744
We've given it a prompt saying generate
a basic HTML image tag for the file

225
00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:13.646
big underscore fish dot png.

226
00:10:13.646 --> 00:10:15.314
Make sure to include an alt tag.

227
00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:18.317
Let's inspect the results of this
to see if we like what we got.

228
00:10:18.517 --> 00:10:22.822
Okay, so this is an image tag
and the source says big fish dot png.

229
00:10:23.189 --> 00:10:25.157
And the all tag says data about fish.

230
00:10:25.157 --> 00:10:27.760
This may be useful
if we were creating a blog post,

231
00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.762
and we wanted to make sure
that our images were findable.

232
00:10:29.762 --> 00:10:31.530
We don't have to just trust this
by looking at it.

233
00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.766
We can actually render
the HTML on the page.

234
00:10:33.766 --> 00:10:36.769
And as we see our image tag works,
rendering the picture of our big fish.

235
00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:39.205
Structure is everywhere.

236
00:10:39.205 --> 00:10:42.308
We often think of structure
as strictly as JSON or

237
00:10:42.508 --> 00:10:45.511
simple regular expressions
and things like markdown.

238
00:10:45.511 --> 00:10:48.514
But in this example, we're
going to generate a Tic-Tac-Toe board.

239
00:10:48.648 --> 00:10:51.183
Here's our regex for the Tic-Tac-Toe board.

240
00:10:51.183 --> 00:10:55.421
It is a bit involved, but this defines the
structure of an ASCII Tic-Tac-Toe board.

241
00:10:55.421 --> 00:10:57.390
Let's go ahead and generate an example.

242
00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.325
Well, once again, create our generator.

243
00:10:59.325 --> 00:11:01.227
And we will generate our Tic-Tac-Toe board.

244
00:11:01.227 --> 00:11:03.396
Notice that in the prompt,
I've showed the model

245
00:11:03.396 --> 00:11:06.098
an example of what I think does
Tic-Tac-Toe board should look like,

246
00:11:06.098 --> 00:11:08.434
even though we're guaranteed
to get the structure we want,

247
00:11:08.434 --> 00:11:11.804
it's always helpful to provide examples
in your prompt of the output

248
00:11:11.804 --> 00:11:12.805
you are hoping to get.

249
00:11:12.805 --> 00:11:16.409
Finally, we can print out our Tic-Tac-Toe
board and as you see,

250
00:11:16.409 --> 00:11:19.512
we have a Tic-Tac-Toe
game in progress generated by our LLM.

251
00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:22.715
We've talked at length about JSON,

252
00:11:22.715 --> 00:11:24.583
but if you're a data scientist
or work in machine

253
00:11:24.583 --> 00:11:28.254
learning, you're probably more familiar
with CSV as a file format of choice.

254
00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:31.490
In this example,
we're going to generate a CSV

255
00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:34.960
content straight from the model
and pump it into a pandas dataframe.

256
00:11:35.227 --> 00:11:37.329
Here is the regex that we're going
to be using to generate this.

257
00:11:37.329 --> 00:11:41.133
You can see we have three columns code
amount and cost, which represent

258
00:11:41.133 --> 00:11:42.768
an item code for an inventory item.

259
00:11:42.768 --> 00:11:45.771
The number of that item we have
and the cost of the item per unit.

260
00:11:45.838 --> 00:11:50.009
And then we've specified some properties
of this CSV file that we want to have.

261
00:11:50.009 --> 00:11:54.246
So for example, we can see here
that the code has to be a three character

262
00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:55.047
item code.

263
00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:57.883
Then we have up to two digits
for the amount.

264
00:11:57.883 --> 00:12:01.787
And we represent cost using more digits
and a decimal place to make sure

265
00:12:01.787 --> 00:12:03.889
we always have a proper dollar value.

266
00:12:03.889 --> 00:12:05.991
Here is our simple CSV generator.

267
00:12:05.991 --> 00:12:08.794
And then we can use this
to create our CSV out.

268
00:12:08.794 --> 00:12:10.963
Notice
we just briefly described our CSV file

269
00:12:10.963 --> 00:12:13.466
here, and we'll see how well the model
can do with just that in the prompt.

270
00:12:13.466 --> 00:12:16.469
Rather than print out
this string representation of this result.

271
00:12:16.502 --> 00:12:20.306
We can actually pipe this directly
into Pandas using Python string IO.

272
00:12:20.372 --> 00:12:23.342
And as you can see,
it's successfully created a CSV file

273
00:12:23.342 --> 00:12:26.645
that we can directly send
from the LLM to Pandas.

274
00:12:26.712 --> 00:12:28.080
This is incredibly useful

275
00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:29.815
if you're doing any kind of data
processing

276
00:12:29.815 --> 00:12:32.384
that's going to be part
of a data science workflow.

277
00:12:32.384 --> 00:12:34.653
In this next part,
we're going to talk about implementing

278
00:12:34.653 --> 00:12:38.624
structure for GSM8K,
which is a common LLM evaluation benchmark

279
00:12:38.624 --> 00:12:42.328
that uses grade school questions
to see if LLMs can answer them correctly.

280
00:12:42.561 --> 00:12:45.197
We're also going to talk about ways
we can make regex easier.

281
00:12:45.197 --> 00:12:48.100
Here's an example of a common GSM8K
type question.

282
00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:50.703
Tom has three cucumbers.
Joe gives him two more.

283
00:12:50.703 --> 00:12:52.037
How many does Tom have?

284
00:12:52.037 --> 00:12:53.906
This is what's provided to the model
in the prompt.

285
00:12:53.906 --> 00:12:55.708
It then follows up
with the reasoning step.

286
00:12:55.708 --> 00:12:57.643
Here's
the model thinking about the problem.

287
00:12:57.643 --> 00:13:00.479
Tom started with three cucumbers,
then received two more.

288
00:13:00.479 --> 00:13:02.014
This means he has five cucumbers.

289
00:13:02.014 --> 00:13:04.049
Finally, the model is required to answer.

290
00:13:04.049 --> 00:13:06.118
So the answer is five.

291
00:13:06.118 --> 00:13:07.853
Notice
that even though this is plain text,

292
00:13:07.853 --> 00:13:10.122
there is still a very clear structure
here.

293
00:13:10.122 --> 00:13:11.757
We're going to prompt the model
with the question

294
00:13:11.757 --> 00:13:13.392
and hope that it follows
through with the correct

295
00:13:13.392 --> 00:13:15.561
reasoning and most importantly,
the correct answer,

296
00:13:15.561 --> 00:13:18.397
all following this format
that we can enforce in the model.

297
00:13:18.397 --> 00:13:22.201
Of course, writing a regular expression
for this problem would be very tricky.

298
00:13:22.368 --> 00:13:25.371
Thankfully, in Outlines
we have a domain-specific language

299
00:13:25.371 --> 00:13:29.508
that allows us to very easily represent
regular expressions in a language

300
00:13:29.508 --> 00:13:30.810
that's easy to understand.

301
00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:34.280
You can see we're using it by importing
sentence and digit from Outlines types

302
00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.482
and the DSLs to regex function.

303
00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:38.517
Reasoning here starts
with the phrase reasoning,

304
00:13:38.517 --> 00:13:40.252
just like it does in this example.

305
00:13:40.252 --> 00:13:43.155
And then we're going to add a sentence
that repeats 1 to 2 times.

306
00:13:43.155 --> 00:13:45.257
That means we're
The statement has to start with reasoning,

307
00:13:45.257 --> 00:13:47.793
and we're allowing the reasoning
to go on for 1 or 2 sentences

308
00:13:47.793 --> 00:13:48.994
which have already been predefined.

309
00:13:48.994 --> 00:13:51.130
So no regular expression
required from you.

310
00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.065
Then the answer needs to lead with:

311
00:13:53.065 --> 00:13:55.434
So the answer is colon space.

312
00:13:55.434 --> 00:13:58.437
And then we need to have a digit
that's between 1 and 4 digits long.

313
00:13:58.771 --> 00:14:00.573
See, we use the digit here

314
00:14:00.573 --> 00:14:03.475
and just repeat it 1 to 4 times.
Very straightforward.

315
00:14:03.475 --> 00:14:06.478
Finally, we're going to put this
all two regex to see what it looks like.

316
00:14:06.512 --> 00:14:08.981
I'm certainly glad
I did not have to write that by hand.

317
00:14:08.981 --> 00:14:12.585
Next, we'll build our regex generator
by passing in this new regex we made

318
00:14:12.718 --> 00:14:13.786
just as we have before.

319
00:14:14.753 --> 00:14:17.656
Now we're ready
to test out how smart our model really is.

320
00:14:17.656 --> 00:14:19.225
We're going to give it this question right
here.

321
00:14:19.225 --> 00:14:21.760
Sally has five apples, then receives
two more.

322
00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:23.028
How many does Sally have?

323
00:14:23.028 --> 00:14:24.697
Now we can look at the prompt
we're going to use

324
00:14:24.697 --> 00:14:26.465
to send this question to the model.

325
00:14:26.465 --> 00:14:29.034
In the prompt
we explain the structure of the problem.

326
00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:32.104
And we talk about how this is going
to work and provide an example.

327
00:14:32.271 --> 00:14:36.342
And then we just put our question into
template keeping format with this prompt.

328
00:14:36.342 --> 00:14:39.645
So we would expect the next
natural step would be to output reasoning.

329
00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:40.746
And then a solution.

330
00:14:40.746 --> 00:14:43.249
And finally, we can see how the model does.

331
00:14:43.249 --> 00:14:47.052
As we can see the model correctly reasons
that Sally had five apples.

332
00:14:47.052 --> 00:14:50.489
They received two more, which means
she has five plus two equals seven apples.

333
00:14:50.489 --> 00:14:52.224
So the answer is seven.

334
00:14:52.224 --> 00:14:54.627
All in a structure
we can easily understand and parse.

335
00:14:54.627 --> 00:14:56.095
Of course we could have done this in JSON,

336
00:14:56.095 --> 00:14:59.331
but it's always worth trying with sort
of more natural forms of structure

337
00:14:59.365 --> 00:15:00.833
we find every day.

338
00:15:00.833 --> 00:15:03.569
Finally, we're going to leave you
with a very fun project.

339
00:15:03.569 --> 00:15:07.640
You're going to build your very own
hot dog versus not a hot dog classifier.

340
00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:10.743
We're going to start with some basic
boilerplate code to get us started.

341
00:15:11.076 --> 00:15:14.313
This loads in a vision model,
which is a multimodal model

342
00:15:14.313 --> 00:15:17.216
that allows us to actually use images
with our prompt.

343
00:15:17.216 --> 00:15:19.184
Because your task is to add the structure.

344
00:15:19.184 --> 00:15:23.322
We're going to include, very simple
use of outlines, dot text method.

345
00:15:23.455 --> 00:15:27.126
This simply returns and unstructured
result from the model, allowing you

346
00:15:27.159 --> 00:15:29.728
to complete the rest of the work. Next,
we'll look at our prompts,

347
00:15:29.728 --> 00:15:31.597
which will help us understand
the task at hand.

